<!-- @after 7. Deep Dive: Write → Buffer → Flush (15 min) :: animation -->
### Follow a batch through a 413

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated><span class="stepper-title">Durable events, one oversized batch</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="viz-grid" data-cols="3"><div class="node" data-el="feature" data-art="code" data-art-role="client"><strong>Checkout feature</strong><small data-text="featureV">track(…)</small></div><div class="node" data-el="chain" data-art="gate" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Interceptors</strong><small data-text="chainV">consent, PII, sampling</small></div><div class="node" data-el="queue" data-art="database" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Room queue</strong><small data-text="queueV">events</small></div></div><div class="viz-grid" data-cols="3"><div class="node" data-el="sched" data-art="clock" data-art-role="queue"><strong>FlushScheduler</strong><small data-text="schedV">size and age</small></div><div class="node" data-el="worker" data-art="worker" data-art-role="queue"><strong>FlushWorker</strong><small data-text="workerV">idle</small></div><div class="node" data-el="api" data-art="server" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Events API</strong><small data-text="apiV">POST /v1/events</small></div></div><div class="node is-key" data-el="event" data-art="signal" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>What happens</strong><small data-text="eventV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
  {
    "label": "track() returns at once",
    "caption": "A feature calls Analytics.track. The work is launched on the SDK’s supervised IO scope, so the call returns immediately and the caller never waits for disk or network.",
    "add": [
      "feature",
      "chain",
      "queue",
      "sched",
      "worker",
      "api",
      "event"
    ],
    "state": {
      "feature": "active"
    },
    "text": {
      "eventV": "caller not blocked"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Interceptors run",
    "caption": "The consent, redaction and sampling interceptors run in order. Any of them can drop the event, and a property such as email becomes <redacted>.",
    "state": {
      "feature": "done",
      "chain": "done"
    },
    "text": {
      "eventV": "nothing stored without consent"
    },
    "handoff": {
      "from": "feature",
      "to": "chain",
      "kind": "parcel",
      "label": "event"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Durable write",
    "caption": "The event is written to the Room queue, so it survives a crash or a kill. The queue passes its batch threshold, and the scheduler enqueues the unique analytics-flush work with KEEP.",
    "state": {
      "queue": "done",
      "sched": "active"
    },
    "text": {
      "queueV": "events waiting",
      "eventV": "KEEP coalesces bursts"
    },
    "handoff": {
      "from": "chain",
      "to": "queue",
      "kind": "rows",
      "label": "event row"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "413: too large",
    "caption": "Once the device is connected, FlushWorker reads a batch and uploads it. The server answers 413: the request is too large, not invalid. The worker halves the batch size and tries again; nothing is deleted.",
    "state": {
      "sched": "done",
      "worker": "active",
      "api": "error"
    },
    "text": {
      "apiV": "413 Content Too Large",
      "workerV": "batch size halved",
      "eventV": "halve, don’t drop"
    },
    "handoff": {
      "from": "queue",
      "to": "worker",
      "kind": "rows",
      "label": "batch"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Delete only after ack",
    "caption": "The half-size batch is accepted, and only then does the worker delete those events from Room. If the process died before the delete, the batch would be sent again and the server would dedupe it.",
    "state": {
      "api": "done"
    },
    "text": {
      "apiV": "200",
      "queueV": "acknowledged rows deleted",
      "eventV": "at-least-once delivery"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Chain a follow-up",
    "caption": "The worker reaches its per-run batch limit with events still queued. A KEEP enqueue made while it is running would be ignored, so it chains one follow-up with APPEND_OR_REPLACE.",
    "state": {
      "worker": "done",
      "event": "done"
    },
    "text": {
      "workerV": "follow-up chained",
      "eventV": "APPEND_OR_REPLACE"
    }
  }
]</script><figcaption>track() never blocks, Room makes every event durable, and the worker deletes only what the server acknowledged. A single event that is still too large is dropped and counted.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after 12. Further Reading :: recap -->
### Say these in the interview

- **`track()` returns at once**; the work moves to a supervised IO scope.
- **Interceptors enforce consent, schema, PII redaction and sampling** before anything is stored.
- **Room is the buffer.** Events survive crashes, kills and offline time, and the queue is bounded by trimming low-priority categories first.
- **Flush with unique work.** `KEEP` coalesces triggers, and the worker chains one follow-up with `APPEND_OR_REPLACE`.
- **Delete only after the server acknowledges a batch.** Delivery is at-least-once, so the server dedupes, for example by a client-generated event UUID.
- **Handle each status code on purpose.** 413 halves the batch, 400 and 422 drop it, and 401, 408, 429 and 5xx retry.
